After extracting into a new private workspace, verify #raw("manifest.json") and
#raw("SHA256SUMS"). The source archive has sorted regular members, normalized
tar ownership/mode/timestamps and gzip timestamp zero. Two builds must agree.
No cache/index/chunk, raw proof, ELF/library, build output, private environment,
license/configuration, credential, runtime identity or ticket is present.

Exact code is preserved rather than silently rewritten for another host. The
original launcher points at its former workspace and sources its private
#raw("env.sh"), which is intentionally absent. Do not execute that historical
launcher against old paths. Establish fresh permitted CPU, loader, user,
closed-source request, new output, source authority, shared abort-latch authority
and resource limits; relocate code deliberately, then hash/review the changed
source, artifact and controls. Reuse the actual shared admission/guard protocol;
never copy an old invocation, ticket, activation grant or runtime receipt.
Changing paths or source requires a truthful new cohort and fingerprints.

The archived #raw("test_source_controls.py") extracts functions through AST and
uses mocked process/service/filesystem observations. Its historical layout test
expects the former active configuration, which is deliberately not archived.
The other 14 tests can run using only the two controller source files; for a
single inert check from #raw("source/controller/"), run
#raw("python -m unittest test_source_controls.Controls.test_inactive_refuses_before_any_observation").
Do not import or start the controller to test packaging. The standalone verifier
reads real compressed bytes only when deliberately invoked after an accepted
fresh run; merely preserving its source does not run that verification.

This is a source checkpoint. It claims neither an extracted fresh-host rebuild
nor fresh runtime qualification, full CI, release publication or goal completion.
